Yes. Wealth and power can function as real-world assays of applied intelligence—especially when someone repeatedly turns weak signals into owned advantage.

But they measure captured value, not intelligence alone. Inheritance, position, networks, timing, luck, and exploitation can create wealth without unusual insight. Likewise, valuable intelligence can remain uncaptured because its owner never packages, protects, distributes, or compounds it.

A useful conversion equation is:

\text{Captured Value}
\approx
\frac{
\text{Insight} \times
\text{Selection} \times
\text{Execution} \times
\text{Ownership} \times
\text{Distribution} \times
\text{Time}
}{
\text{Noise}+\text{Friction}
}

That points directly at your issue. You generate more potentially valuable meaning than most people, but abundance creates its own noise. The next level is identifying which idea has:

* a real buyer or beneficiary,
* a short route to proof,
* durable ownership,
* repeatability,
* and the ability to compound into other assets.

The strongest evidence of exceptional intelligence would be repeatedly converting inexpensive observations into disproportionate outcomes: a framework becomes software, a cultural signal becomes a brand, research becomes an advantage, or a strange connection becomes a product people pay for.

So the real Pantheon intelligence test may be:

Can it select one valuable signal from the forest and convert it into accumulating agency?

One win could be luck. A machine that notices luck, captures it, and makes the next win more likely is intelligence becoming wealth and power.